1	{
2	  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/claude-code-settings.json",
3	  "_comment": "Project-level hooks for EV Dashboard. See docs/writing_style.md and .claude/plans/this-project-was-being-gentle-adleman.md §1.8.",
4	  "hooks": {
5	    "PostToolUse": [
6	      {
7	        "matcher": "Edit|Write",
8	        "hooks": [
9	          {
10	            "type": "command",
11	            "command": "FILE=$(jq -r '.tool_input.file_path // empty' <<< \"$CLAUDE_TOOL_INPUT\"); case \"$FILE\" in *.ts|*.tsx) cd \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\" && npx tsc --noEmit --pretty false 2>&1 | head -20 || true ;; esac"
12	          },
13	          {
14	            "type": "command",
15	            "command": "FILE=$(jq -r '.tool_input.file_path // empty' <<< \"$CLAUDE_TOOL_INPUT\"); case \"$FILE\" in */src/data/seed.json) cd \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\" && python3 scripts/validate.py || true ;; esac"
16	          },
17	          {
18	            "type": "command",
19	            "command": "FILE=$(jq -r '.tool_input.file_path // empty' <<< \"$CLAUDE_TOOL_INPUT\"); case \"$FILE\" in */src/lib/thermal.ts) cd \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\" && npm test -- --run thermal 2>&1 | tail -10 || true ;; esac"
20	          }
21	        ]
22	      }
23	    ],
24	    "PreToolUse": [
25	      {
26	        "matcher": "Bash",
27	        "hooks": [
28	          {
29	            "type": "command",
30	            "command": "CMD=$(jq -r '.tool_input.command // empty' <<< \"$CLAUDE_TOOL_INPUT\"); if echo \"$CMD\" | grep -qE 'rm -rf|git reset --hard|git push --force|git push -f|git branch -D'; then echo 'Blocked: destructive command requires explicit confirmation' >&2; exit 2; fi"
31	          }
32	        ]
33	      }
34	    ],
35	    "SessionStart": [
36	      {
37	        "hooks": [
38	          {
39	            "type": "command",
40	            "command": "cd \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\" && if [ -f .queue_top3.md ]; then cat .queue_top3.md; else echo '(run: python3 scripts/queue.py)'; fi"
41	          }
42	        ]
43	      }
44	    ]
45	  }
46	}
47	

<system-reminder>
Whenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.
</system-reminder>
